WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.074 --> 00:00:13.336
Welcome to Finora — an all-in-one business management and accounting platform, built for companies in the United Arab Emirates.

2
00:00:13.376 --> 00:00:23.884
In this tutorial we will walk through every part of the system, from signing in, to invoicing, purchasing, banking, inventory, VAT, and reports.

3
00:00:24.599 --> 00:00:27.306
Finora is real double-entry accounting.

4
00:00:27.346 --> 00:00:38.929
Every invoice, bill, payment and expense you record automatically posts a balanced journal entry, and every financial report is calculated directly from that ledger.

5
00:00:38.969 --> 00:00:42.099
Nothing on a report is ever typed in by hand.

6
00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:45.231
New businesses use the Register tab.

7
00:00:45.271 --> 00:00:51.227
You enter your name, your organisation name and base currency, an email and a password.

8
00:00:51.267 --> 00:00:59.980
Finora then creates your chart of accounts, UAE VAT rates, document numbering and a default bank account for you automatically.

9
00:01:00.642 --> 00:01:04.843
Existing users simply sign in with their email and password.

10
00:01:04.883 --> 00:01:09.722
We will sign in as the owner of our demo company, Finora Trading LLC.

11
00:01:17.673 --> 00:01:22.242
The first time you sign in, Finora offers a short product tour.

12
00:01:22.282 --> 00:01:23.486
Let us follow it.

13
00:01:23.526 --> 00:01:35.633
The sidebar on the left holds every module, grouped the way the work is grouped: Sales, Purchases, Operations, Banking, Inventory, Accountant, Compliance, and Manage.

14
00:01:36.293 --> 00:01:39.178
At the top left is the organisation switcher.

15
00:01:39.218 --> 00:01:44.120
It shows which company’s books you are in, your role, and the base currency.

16
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:48.932
If you keep books for more than one company, you switch between them here.

17
00:01:48.972 --> 00:01:51.663
Data is never mixed between organisations.

18
00:01:52.327 --> 00:01:58.828
The search box finds any invoice, customer, bill, vendor or item by name or number.

19
00:01:58.868 --> 00:02:02.297
You can open it from anywhere with Ctrl + K.

20
00:02:02.951 --> 00:02:06.392
The New button is the one you will use most.

21
00:02:06.432 --> 00:02:15.491
It starts any document — a customer, quote, invoice, bill, expense, transfer or journal — from anywhere in the app.

22
00:02:16.162 --> 00:02:24.745
The bell collects notifications: approvals waiting for you, invoices that became overdue, and results of scheduled jobs.

23
00:02:24.785 --> 00:02:29.992
The avatar menu holds settings, the audit trail, this tour, and sign out.

24
00:02:30.661 --> 00:02:35.421
And finally, the numbers on the dashboard all come straight from the ledger.

25
00:02:35.461 --> 00:02:38.011
Let us close the tour and try the search.

26
00:02:45.843 --> 00:02:58.207
The dashboard greets you with four key figures: cash on hand across your bank and cash accounts, receivables your customers owe you, payables you owe vendors, and net profit for the year to date.

27
00:02:58.247 --> 00:03:01.643
Each card is clickable and opens the report behind it.

28
00:03:02.309 --> 00:03:05.927
Below that, an alert highlights overdue invoices.

29
00:03:05.967 --> 00:03:18.249
Then you see a six-month cash chart, invoice value by status, recent invoices and bills, items that need attention, your top customers, and recent payments received.

30
00:03:18.902 --> 00:03:20.848
Two shortcuts sit at the top.

31
00:03:20.888 --> 00:03:28.862
Run scheduled jobs generates any recurring invoices, bills and journals that are due, and refreshes overdue statuses.

32
00:03:28.902 --> 00:03:31.602
New Invoice jumps straight to invoicing.

33
00:03:36.215 --> 00:03:39.936
Everything on the sales side starts with a customer.

34
00:03:39.976 --> 00:03:47.314
The Customers list shows each contact with email, phone, tax registration number, currency and status.

35
00:03:47.354 --> 00:03:52.304
You can export the list, or import customers in bulk from a CSV file.

36
00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:54.977
Let us add a new customer.

37
00:03:55.017 --> 00:03:56.946
Display name is required.

38
00:03:56.986 --> 00:04:10.332
You can add the company, contact person, email, phone, the fifteen-digit TRN, and the tax treatment: VAT registered, not registered, GCC, export, out of scope, or exempt.

39
00:04:10.372 --> 00:04:16.160
The tax treatment decides how VAT is applied on this customer’s documents.

40
00:04:16.828 --> 00:04:21.221
Set the billing address, currency and payment terms in days.

41
00:04:21.261 --> 00:04:28.683
An opening balance, if the customer already owes you money, is posted against Opening Balance Equity.

42
00:04:28.723 --> 00:04:31.048
Save, and the customer is ready.

43
00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:37.974
Open any customer to see their profile, outstanding balance, invoices and payment history.

44
00:04:38.014 --> 00:04:46.170
From here you can print a statement of account, open their customer portal, record a payment, or raise a new invoice.

45
00:04:50.811 --> 00:04:53.485
A typical sale can start with a quote.

46
00:04:53.525 --> 00:04:55.629
Quotes never touch the ledger.

47
00:04:55.669 --> 00:04:59.701
Let us quote one industrial pump for Al Noor Hospitality.

48
00:04:59.741 --> 00:05:03.131
For a quote, the second date is the expiry date.

49
00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:07.753
The quote is saved as a draft.

50
00:05:07.793 --> 00:05:09.484
Send it to the customer.

51
00:05:09.524 --> 00:05:18.643
When they agree, mark it accepted — and then convert it, either into a sales order for fulfilment, or straight into an invoice.

52
00:05:18.683 --> 00:05:21.240
Here we convert it to a sales order.

53
00:05:21.941 --> 00:05:24.186
A sales order is a confirmed order.

54
00:05:24.226 --> 00:05:32.348
From it you create delivery notes to ship the goods — posting a delivery reduces stock — and an invoice to bill the customer.

55
00:05:32.388 --> 00:05:35.221
Delivery notes are listed on their own page.

56
00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:39.411
Now the most important document: the invoice.

57
00:05:39.451 --> 00:05:51.870
The Invoices list shows outstanding, overdue and draft totals, and you can filter by status: draft, sent, partially paid, paid, overdue, void or written off.

58
00:05:52.592 --> 00:05:53.707
Click New Invoice.

59
00:05:53.747 --> 00:06:01.862
Choose the customer, the invoice date and the due date, and an optional reference such as the customer’s purchase order number.

60
00:06:05.473 --> 00:06:06.490
Add line items.

61
00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:11.427
Linking an item fills in the description, price and tax automatically.

62
00:06:11.467 --> 00:06:18.621
Here we bill three hours of installation service, and add a second line for two safety equipment kits.

63
00:06:18.661 --> 00:06:22.852
The amount, and the five percent VAT, calculate as you type.

64
00:06:23.567 --> 00:06:27.126
The summary on the right shows subtotal, tax and total.

65
00:06:27.166 --> 00:06:35.831
Finora stores money as whole fils — never floating-point numbers — and the server recalculates every total before anything is posted.

66
00:06:35.871 --> 00:06:41.067
The panel below shows exactly what will hit the ledger when the invoice is sent.

67
00:06:41.748 --> 00:06:45.726
Save draft keeps the invoice with no accounting effect.

68
00:06:45.766 --> 00:06:51.863
Save and send finalises it, gives it the next invoice number, and posts the journal.

69
00:06:51.903 --> 00:06:53.398
Let us save and send.

70
00:06:54.061 --> 00:06:55.855
Here is the posted invoice.

71
00:06:55.895 --> 00:07:08.491
On the right is its journal entry: accounts receivable is debited with the full amount, sales and service revenue are credited with the net amount, and output VAT is credited separately.

72
00:07:08.531 --> 00:07:10.801
Debits equal credits, to the fils.

73
00:07:11.501 --> 00:07:22.126
From here you can print or save a PDF, email the invoice, record a payment, issue a credit note, write off the balance as bad debt, or void it.

74
00:07:22.166 --> 00:07:26.751
Voiding never deletes anything — it posts a reversing journal.

75
00:07:27.408 --> 00:07:30.006
Let us look at the printable version.

76
00:07:30.046 --> 00:07:38.064
It is a UAE tax invoice showing your TRN, the customer’s details, each line with its VAT, and the total in words.

77
00:07:38.104 --> 00:07:42.628
Use your browser’s print dialog to print it or save it as a PDF.

78
00:07:47.231 --> 00:07:50.546
When the customer pays, click Record payment.

79
00:07:50.586 --> 00:08:00.015
Choose the amount, date, payment mode — bank transfer, cash, cheque, card or online — and the bank account the money went into.

80
00:08:00.055 --> 00:08:03.371
The payment is allocated against the invoice.

81
00:08:04.032 --> 00:08:09.905
Saving posts a second journal: the bank is debited and accounts receivable credited.

82
00:08:09.945 --> 00:08:14.482
The invoice balance drops to zero and its status changes to Paid.

83
00:08:15.146 --> 00:08:20.993
If you receive more than the invoice balance, the extra is kept as a customer advance.

84
00:08:21.033 --> 00:08:24.006
All receipts appear under Payments Received.

85
00:08:24.656 --> 00:08:28.106
Credit notes handle returns and price corrections.

86
00:08:28.146 --> 00:08:36.066
A credit note reverses revenue and output VAT, and can be applied to an open invoice, or refunded to the customer.

87
00:08:40.721 --> 00:08:42.429
Purchasing mirrors sales.

88
00:08:42.469 --> 00:08:46.346
Vendors are your suppliers, managed just like customers.

89
00:08:46.386 --> 00:08:50.892
A purchase order commits spend without touching accounts payable.

90
00:08:50.932 --> 00:08:52.080
Let us raise one.

91
00:08:52.734 --> 00:08:57.334
We order twenty industrial brushes from Industrial Supplier LLC.

92
00:08:57.374 --> 00:09:01.466
The purchase price comes from the item, and VAT is added.

93
00:09:01.506 --> 00:09:02.554
Save the order.

94
00:09:05.933 --> 00:09:07.830
Send the order to the vendor.

95
00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:13.043
If you agreed it by phone instead, Confirm marks it confirmed without sending.

96
00:09:13.716 --> 00:09:16.454
When the goods arrive, click Receive goods.

97
00:09:16.494 --> 00:09:18.327
This creates a goods receipt.

98
00:09:18.367 --> 00:09:23.496
Posting the receipt increases stock in the warehouse at the purchase-order cost.

99
00:09:24.157 --> 00:09:26.881
The purchase order is now marked received.

100
00:09:26.921 --> 00:09:31.226
When the vendor’s invoice arrives, go to Bills and click New Bill.

101
00:09:31.266 --> 00:09:36.887
Choose the vendor, and type the vendor’s own invoice number so you can match it later.

102
00:09:37.533 --> 00:09:40.720
Add the twenty brushes at the agreed cost.

103
00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:47.407
Posting a bill debits inventory or expense and input VAT, and credits accounts payable.

104
00:09:47.447 --> 00:09:48.483
Save and post.

105
00:09:50.705 --> 00:09:52.831
Pay the bill with Record payment.

106
00:09:52.871 --> 00:09:56.112
This debits accounts payable and credits the bank.

107
00:09:56.152 --> 00:10:03.005
Vendor credits work like customer credit notes, and all outgoing payments are listed under Payments Made.

108
00:10:07.597 --> 00:10:14.283
Expenses record day-to-day spending that does not need a vendor bill — utilities, fuel, subscriptions.

109
00:10:14.323 --> 00:10:20.417
Click Record expense, choose the date, the merchant, the expense account, and the net amount.

110
00:10:23.487 --> 00:10:31.814
Pick the VAT rate and how it was paid: from a bank account, cash, card — or by an employee who needs to be reimbursed.

111
00:10:31.854 --> 00:10:37.417
Employee claims can be approved, rejected, and reimbursed later from this list.

112
00:10:42.027 --> 00:10:44.268
Banking starts with your accounts.

113
00:10:44.308 --> 00:10:51.047
Each bank or cash account is mapped to a ledger account, so its balance always agrees with the books.

114
00:10:51.087 --> 00:10:55.476
Move money between them with New transfer, including any bank fee.

115
00:10:56.138 --> 00:10:59.325
Transactions shows your bank statement lines.

116
00:10:59.365 --> 00:11:04.992
Import a statement with Import CSV: choose the bank account and paste the file.

117
00:11:05.032 --> 00:11:10.658
Columns are matched by header — date, description, reference, debit and credit.

118
00:11:10.698 --> 00:11:16.038
Duplicates are detected and skipped, and your bank rules run automatically.

119
00:11:16.693 --> 00:11:20.073
For each uncategorised line you have two choices.

120
00:11:20.113 --> 00:11:28.032
Find match links a deposit or payment to an existing invoice, bill or payment, so no duplicate journal is created.

121
00:11:28.072 --> 00:11:31.452
Categorize posts the line directly to an account.

122
00:11:31.492 --> 00:11:35.083
Here we file the Google Ads payment under Marketing.

123
00:11:35.742 --> 00:11:37.257
Rules do this for you.

124
00:11:37.297 --> 00:11:45.741
A rule says: if the description contains certain text, or the amount meets a condition, then categorise to this account.

125
00:11:45.781 --> 00:11:48.852
Rules run in priority order on every import.

126
00:11:49.517 --> 00:11:51.099
Finally, reconciliation.

127
00:11:51.139 --> 00:12:00.837
Pick the bank account, enter the statement period and the closing balance from your bank statement, and tick the transactions that appear on it.

128
00:12:00.877 --> 00:12:07.667
When the difference reaches zero, click Finish reconciliation, and those lines are marked reconciled.

129
00:12:12.273 --> 00:12:16.209
Items are the products and services you buy and sell.

130
00:12:16.249 --> 00:12:19.435
Goods can track inventory; services cannot.

131
00:12:19.475 --> 00:12:28.363
Each item carries a SKU, sales price, purchase cost, tax, reorder level, and the revenue and cost accounts it posts to.

132
00:12:29.024 --> 00:12:30.849
Stock is held in warehouses.

133
00:12:30.889 --> 00:12:40.244
Every receipt, delivery, adjustment and transfer creates an immutable stock movement with a running balance — this is your stock audit trail.

134
00:12:40.905 --> 00:12:44.815
Use adjustments after a stock count, or for damage and loss.

135
00:12:44.855 --> 00:12:52.123
You enter the counted quantity, and the difference is valued at weighted-average cost and posted to the ledger.

136
00:12:52.163 --> 00:12:56.535
Transfers move stock between warehouses without changing the total.

137
00:12:57.200 --> 00:13:03.422
Valuation shows the quantity on hand, average cost and total value of every tracked item.

138
00:13:03.462 --> 00:13:08.630
This figure always agrees with the Inventory account on the balance sheet.

139
00:13:13.218 --> 00:13:18.635
The chart of accounts lists every ledger account with its code, type and balance.

140
00:13:18.675 --> 00:13:26.518
System accounts such as receivables, payables and VAT are protected, and any account with activity cannot be deleted.

141
00:13:27.183 --> 00:13:31.364
Journals shows every entry in the ledger, and where it came from.

142
00:13:31.404 --> 00:13:38.833
Posted journals can never be edited — corrections are made with reversing entries, so the history is always intact.

143
00:13:39.487 --> 00:13:44.407
Accountants can post manual journals, for example to accrue an expense.

144
00:13:44.447 --> 00:13:50.905
Choose the accounts, enter debits and credits, and Finora refuses to post until they balance.

145
00:13:50.945 --> 00:13:57.612
Here we accrue five thousand dirhams of September rent: debit Rent, and credit Accounts Payable.

146
00:13:57.652 --> 00:14:00.546
The footer confirms the entry is balanced.

147
00:14:09.186 --> 00:14:11.832
Debits equal credits, so we can post.

148
00:14:11.872 --> 00:14:23.016
Recurring journals repeat an entry weekly, monthly, quarterly or yearly — such as depreciation — and the engine guarantees exactly one entry per run date.

149
00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:30.330
The general ledger lists every line posted to each account in a date range, with a running balance.

150
00:14:30.370 --> 00:14:33.980
Filter by account, and export to CSV for your auditor.

151
00:14:38.586 --> 00:14:42.934
Taxes and VAT prepares your UAE VAT return for any period.

152
00:14:42.974 --> 00:14:55.344
Output VAT on your sales and input VAT on your purchases and expenses are grouped by treatment: standard rated, zero rated, exempt, out of scope and reverse charge.

153
00:14:55.384 --> 00:15:01.246
The net amount is what you pay to, or reclaim from, the Federal Tax Authority.

154
00:15:01.914 --> 00:15:09.694
Every figure drills down to the underlying documents, and Export audit file downloads the full VAT transaction listing.

155
00:15:14.317 --> 00:15:21.180
Reports are grouped into financial statements, receivables and payables, and sales and purchase analysis.

156
00:15:21.220 --> 00:15:27.427
None of them can be edited — they are calculated from posted journals every time you open them.

157
00:15:28.089 --> 00:15:39.569
The Profit and Loss shows revenue, cost of goods sold, gross profit, operating expenses and net profit for any date range, with an optional comparison period.

158
00:15:40.236 --> 00:15:47.073
The Balance Sheet shows assets, liabilities and equity as of any date — and it always balances.

159
00:15:47.113 --> 00:15:53.806
The Trial Balance lists every account’s debit or credit balance, and its totals always agree.

160
00:15:54.463 --> 00:16:04.236
Aged receivables groups what customers owe into current, one to thirty, thirty-one to sixty, sixty-one to ninety, and over ninety days.

161
00:16:04.276 --> 00:16:07.217
Use it every week to chase overdue money.

162
00:16:07.257 --> 00:16:09.253
Every report has Export CSV.

163
00:16:13.836 --> 00:16:28.241
Automation lets you build workflows: when something happens — an invoice is sent, a bill is created, stock runs low — and a condition is met, Finora sends a notification, an email, or requests an approval.

164
00:16:28.281 --> 00:16:34.866
Reminders email customers before invoices fall due, and webhooks push events to other systems.

165
00:16:35.513 --> 00:16:43.679
When approvals are switched on in Settings, any document above your approval threshold is held until a manager approves it.

166
00:16:43.719 --> 00:16:47.883
Approval requests appear here, with approve and reject buttons.

167
00:16:48.552 --> 00:16:52.062
Every customer and vendor gets a secure portal link.

168
00:16:52.102 --> 00:17:00.120
Customers can see their invoices and statements, accept quotes, and pay online — payments post straight to your books.

169
00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:02.852
Vendors can acknowledge purchase orders.

170
00:17:02.892 --> 00:17:05.652
Copy a link, and send it to your contact.

171
00:17:06.310 --> 00:17:15.875
This is what your customer sees: the total amount due, their invoices with a Pay button, open quotes they can accept, and their payment history.

172
00:17:15.915 --> 00:17:18.410
They never see your internal accounts.

173
00:17:23.020 --> 00:17:32.583
The AI assistant answers questions about your books in plain language: which invoices are unpaid, who your top customers are, how your cash looks.

174
00:17:32.623 --> 00:17:33.635
It is read-only.

175
00:17:33.675 --> 00:17:41.200
If you ask it to do something, such as send payment reminders, it proposes the action and waits for you to confirm.

176
00:17:41.862 --> 00:17:59.202
Settings is where the owner configures the organisation: the company profile and TRN, VAT registration, designated zone, the period lock date that protects closed months, approval workflows and threshold, and the inventory valuation policy.

177
00:17:59.872 --> 00:18:10.507
Users and roles lets you invite colleagues and give each one a role: owner, admin, accountant, sales, purchases, inventory, employee or viewer.

178
00:18:10.547 --> 00:18:13.942
Each role only sees and does what it needs to.

179
00:18:14.599 --> 00:18:26.969
The remaining tabs manage tax rates, currencies and exchange rates, document number prefixes, the email log, and the audit trail — a permanent record of who did what, and when.

180
00:18:28.636 --> 00:18:41.644
Health runs Finora’s built-in accounting checks: every journal balances, the trial balance agrees, the balance sheet balances, document balances are consistent, and stock matches its movements.

181
00:18:41.684 --> 00:18:44.146
All green means your books are sound.

182
00:18:44.828 --> 00:18:47.140
That completes our tour of Finora.

183
00:18:47.180 --> 00:19:00.286
The routine is simple: record sales and purchases as they happen, import your bank statement and categorise it, reconcile each month, and let the reports and the VAT return build themselves.

184
00:19:00.326 --> 00:19:01.877
Thank you for watching.

